proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "Replace this field with completed_targets below. After highlighted cubes were lifted and returned, the color-and-position prefix definition failed to count genuine completed pickups. It consequently left already handled targets eligible and sometimes remained at zero after a completed lift. This breaks SUFFICIENCY and PREDICTABILITY. The recorded executions do not follow the prescribed red–green–blue, increasing-y order. A scalar prefix count cannot represent their progress without an independently justified target order."
    },
    {
      "name": "completed_targets",
      "value_type": "text",
      "description": "The goal requires picking up each individually highlighted cube, but after a cube is returned its completed pickup is no longer visible. Replace the prefix count with the identities of highlighted cubes whose completed lifts have been observed, independent of pickup order. Assign stable identifiers to the highlighted cubes in highlight_frame by increasing world y, then increasing world x, numbered starting at 1; this numbering identifies cubes and does not prescribe pickup order. Store completed identifiers as a sorted list serialized as text, initially \"[]\". Add an identifier at the first cycle showing that cube held off the table with TCP approximately z=0.15 after a pickup lift. Never add it at the grasp or when the lift is merely commanded. Preserve membership after release, return, and retreat, and never add an identity twice. Example: \"[1, 3]\". Annotate every actual completed highlighted pickup, including those previously excluded by the erroneous prefix rule. This repairs the historical-progress component of SUFFICIENCY and PREDICTABILITY without retaining a redundant count."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "Remove this field and its fixed-order prefix interpretation; use completed_targets instead. The previous instruction excluded visibly completed pickups solely because their identities were not next in an unsupported ordering. This made memory updates and later target selection inconsistent with the execution."
    },
    {
      "name": "completed_targets",
      "description": "After highlighted cubes have been returned, the present scene cannot establish which were previously lifted, so preserve completed identities rather than an ordered-prefix count. From highlight_frame, number only the individually highlighted cubes by increasing world y, then increasing world x, starting at 1. These are stable identity labels, not pickup priorities. Initialize the text value to \"[]\". Match a held cube to its original highlighted identity using position, color, and this cycle's motion frames. When this cycle visibly shows a completed pickup lift of that identity, held off the table with TCP approximately z=0.15, add its identifier and serialize the identifiers in increasing numeric order, for example \"[1, 3]\". Add any newly completed highlighted identity regardless of selection order. Preserve the set through release and retreat. Do not add identities for low grasps, commanded lifts, empty grasps, or an action executing no steps. Membership prevents double counting a previously lifted cube still being held. This makes updates recognizable from the available observations and repairs PREDICTABILITY."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "When the highlights disappeared and the hand was ready to leave the button, the prescribed color-and-position order selected a different cube from the recorded approach. The same rule then redirected an already aligned hand away from its recorded grasp target. After returns, erroneous prefix counts also caused previously lifted cubes to be selected again. These are SUFFICIENCY failures, not failures to retain the highlight display. Remove every red–green–blue pickup-priority rule, every increasing-y pickup-priority rule, and every interpretation of progress as an ordered prefix. Use completed_targets to exclude completed identities; its geometric numbering is only an identity convention. Recognize a newly completed lift from current observations before deciding whether additional targets remain. Return a held completed cube at the current TCP x,y to z=0.01 and open when an uncompleted highlighted target remains; retain the existing open-hand vertical retreat before lateral travel. Keep the final highlighted cube held. An open hand already hovering within the existing alignment tolerance above an uncompleted highlighted cube should descend to that cube at z=0.02 and close, rather than redirecting because of color priority. Preserve the calibrated edge-aligned orientation and existing lift rules. There is still an unresolved selection ambiguity when several uncompleted highlighted cubes are available and the hand is not already aligned over one: the supplied goal gives no order, and the complete executions contradict the previous ordering without establishing a replacement observable ordering rule. Do not invent episode-specific exceptions or encode future recorded actions into memory. Matching the recorded free-choice approach waypoints requires an independently supplied, runtime-observable selection rule, or an evaluation that accepts alternative valid target orders. Separately, a correctly selected cube was localized more than 1 cm away because its top-face plane was left unspecified. Specify a resting-cube top-face plane approximately 0.04 m above the tabletop for the cubes shown here, rather than leaving the surface height unconstrained. Back-project the top-face center onto that plane with the appropriate calibrated camera, refine with the current wrist view when available, and retain the separately specified TCP hover and grasp heights. Do not project a top-face center onto the tabletop or treat the grasp TCP height as the cube's top height.",
  "refused": "Do not pass on the proposed rule that an out-of-order highlighted lift must remain uncounted. That proposal follows the defective prefix convention, whereas the goal requires all highlighted cubes to be picked up and the full executions visibly complete those lifts. Likewise, reports describing a later pickup as the first pickup, or claiming that a blue cube should follow only one completed red pickup, relied on stale counts: the complete executions show two distinct completed pickups before those later approaches. Their progress should be repaired, not used to infer additional priority exceptions. One report also called the approached blue cube green and reversed the identity associated with its proposed coordinates; its identity-specific correction contradicts the images and is excluded. The reports correctly establish that the old ordering is wrong, but they do not establish a general replacement order. No color, spatial, or episode-specific priority is asserted without evidence."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "kind": "delete",
      "field": "lifted_count"
    },
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "completed_targets",
      "value_type": "text",
      "description": "Initially \"[]\". Assign stable identifiers to only the individually highlighted cubes in highlight_frame by increasing original world y, then increasing original world x, numbered starting at 1. This numbering identifies cubes and does not prescribe pickup order; never renumber using positions changed by handling. Store the identifiers of cubes whose completed pickup lifts have been observed, serialized as an ascending numeric list with a comma and space between entries. Add an identifier at the first cycle observing that cube held off the table after a pickup lift with TCP approximately z=0.15, regardless of which other targets have been completed. Do not add it at a low grasp or when a lift is merely commanded. Preserve membership through return, release, and retreat; never add an identifier twice. e.g. \"[1, 3]\""
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "kind": "delete",
      "field": "lifted_count"
    },
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "completed_targets",
      "description": "Initialize to \"[]\". From highlight_frame, number only the individually highlighted cubes by increasing original world y, then increasing original world x, starting at 1. These are stable identity labels, not pickup priorities; never renumber using positions changed by handling. Match a held cube to its highlighted identity using position, color, and this cycle's motion frames. Add its identifier when this cycle visibly shows it held off the table after a completed pickup lift with TCP approximately z=0.15, regardless of selection order. Serialize the identifiers in increasing numeric order with a comma and space between entries. Preserve membership through lowering, release, and retreat. Do not add identities for low grasps, commanded lifts, empty grasps, or an action executing no steps. An identity already present must not be added again when its cube remains held. e.g. \"[1, 3]\""
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "For a resting cube, localize the top-face center at the cube's top height inferred from its geometry, not the tabletop plane or the center of the entire visible silhouette. Refine this estimate with the current calibrated wrist view.",
      "modify_to": "For a resting cube, localize the top-face center, not the center of the entire visible silhouette. For the approximately 4 cm cubes, use a top-face plane approximately 0.04 m above the tabletop, refining the height from visible cube geometry and calibrated views. Back-project the top-face center onto that plane using the appropriate camera calibration and refine with the current calibrated wrist view. Neither the tabletop plane nor the prescribed grasp TCP height is the cube's top-face plane."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Pickup rules: Establish the fixed target order from highlight_frame: red, green, blue, then increasing world y within each color, with increasing world x for ties. Robot-right is negative y and appears on the left of the front image. Match individual identities by position as well as color; do not reorder using positions changed by handling. Interpret lifted_count as excluding the completed prefix, including any newly completed lift observed this cycle. Select the next uncounted identity before testing whether the hand is aligned for descent.",
      "modify_to": "Pickup rules: Recover individually highlighted identities from highlight_frame using the same stable numbering as completed_targets. Robot-right is negative y and appears on the left of the front image. Match identities by position as well as color; numbering is not pickup priority. Exclude every identity in completed_targets, including any newly completed lift observed this cycle. If the open hand at hover is already aligned within roughly 1–1.5 cm above an uncompleted highlighted cube, select that cube and descend rather than redirecting to another target. Otherwise, when ready for lateral travel, select an uncompleted highlighted cube; absent an explicit goal ordering, any such target is eligible. Do not infer a color or spatial pickup priority from the identity numbering."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Only when the open hand is approximately aligned above the selected uncounted target, descend to its center at z=0.02 and close. Allow normal approach error of roughly 1–1.5 cm; if already aligned, descend rather than commanding another hover. An open hand at hover above a returned, counted cube must instead approach the next uncounted target at z=0.15 with fingers open.",
      "modify_to": "Only when the open hand is approximately aligned above the selected uncompleted target, descend to its center at z=0.02 and close. Allow normal approach error of roughly 1–1.5 cm; if already aligned, descend rather than commanding another hover. An open hand at hover above a returned, completed cube must instead approach an uncompleted highlighted target at z=0.15 with fingers open."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Handling additional targets: Once a pickup lift is visibly complete, it counts even if the cube is subsequently returned. If another highlighted target remains and a counted cube is still held at hover height, lower at the current TCP x,y to z=0.01, retaining current orientation, and open on arrival.",
      "modify_to": "Handling additional targets: Once a pickup lift is visibly complete, include its identity in completed_targets even if the cube is subsequently returned. Recognize this completion before deciding whether any uncompleted highlighted target remains. If one remains and a completed cube is still held at hover height, lower at the current TCP x,y to z=0.01, retaining current orientation, and open on arrival."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "When retracted, approach the next uncounted target, not the cube just returned. After lifting the final target, keep it held; success should end the episode without requiring a placement.",
      "modify_to": "When retracted, approach an uncompleted highlighted target, not the cube just returned. When every highlighted identity is completed, keep the final lifted cube held; success should end the episode without requiring a placement."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "The Round 1 fixed-order prefix correction is replaced, rather than reversed into another universal ordering. Across Rounds 2–4, observed completed pickups contradict both its color priority and its same-color priority; this round establishes that these restrictions also suppress genuine memory updates. Stable completed identities preserve actual progress without choosing among the mutually conflicting orders. No replacement color or spatial priority, episode-specific exception, future-action memory, or color relabeling is committed. Free-choice approach waypoints remain underdetermined when several eligible targets exist and the hand is not aligned over one: allowing a valid target does not claim to reproduce the recorded choice. Exact matching there still requires an independently observable selection rule or evaluation accepting alternative valid orders. The approximately 0.04 m cube height is committed as a geometry-dependent estimate, not an unconditional world-z constant. Earlier calibrated localization, object-centered button operation, translation safeguard, and return-and-retreat corrections are retained."
}
